dnl strix-milter wiring for Sendmail — add to your sendmail.mc, then rebuild:
dnl   m4 /etc/mail/sendmail.mc > /etc/mail/sendmail.cf && systemctl restart sendmail
dnl
dnl S= must match MAILSTRIX_MILTER_LISTEN. Note sendmail spells an inet socket
dnl PORT@HOST, the reverse of Postfix's HOST:PORT.
dnl
dnl F= is the failure action, and it is the one setting that matters most:
dnl   F=T  tempfail if the milter is unreachable (defers mail during an outage)
dnl   F=   (empty) accept if unreachable — matches the fail-open posture of the
dnl        rest of this design, and the equivalent of Postfix's
dnl        milter_default_action = accept
dnl
dnl T= timeouts: S=connect/send, R=read reply, E=overall end-of-message. E must
dnl exceed the milter's own -timeout (default 20s) so the milter returns its own
dnl `unknown` verdict rather than sendmail giving up first.

INPUT_MAIL_FILTER(`strix', `S=inet:8081@127.0.0.1, F=, T=S:30s;R:30s;E:5m')

dnl Unix-socket variant (matches the RuntimeDirectory in the systemd unit).
dnl NOTE sendmail spells a unix socket `local:`, not Postfix's `unix:`. Untested
dnl here — only the inet: form above is exercised in this repo's docs; check it
dnl against your sendmail build before relying on it.
dnl INPUT_MAIL_FILTER(`strix', `S=local:/run/strix-milter/milter.sock, F=, T=S:30s;R:30s;E:5m')

dnl Sendmail has no header_checks. To act on the verdict, use a ruleset or run
dnl the check at delivery (procmail/maildrop/Sieve) against X-Mailstrix-Status.
